#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Set up a fast, BOUNDED Rust build environment: sccache, mold, a global Cargo
config, and a size cap on every cache that would otherwise grow without limit.

Run it yourself:

    hyperi-rust-setup            # show what it would do, then ask
    hyperi-rust-setup --check    # show what it would do, change nothing
    hyperi-rust-setup --yes      # no prompt (this is how Ansible calls it)

Not just for provisioning -- a developer on a machine we do not manage should
be able to get the same build environment with one command.

What it sets up
---------------
sccache caches compiled crates, so a rebuild after `cargo clean`, a branch
switch, or a dependency bump reuses work instead of redoing it. mold links
several times faster than the default linker, which dominates the edit-build
-test loop where linking is most of the wall clock.

Neither is exotic; both come from the distro where the distro has them, so
they update with everything else.

How the caches stay bounded
---------------------------
Three caches, three different mechanisms, and only one of them is ours:

- The cargo GLOBAL cache (registry indexes, downloaded sources) bounds itself.
  Cargo tracks last-use and deletes network-fetched files unused for 3 months
  and regenerable files unused for 1 month, on a daily sweep. Nothing is set
  here; a config key would only restate the defaults.
- sccache and ccache evict LRU against a byte ceiling read from the environment
  at startup. This script writes that environment.
- Build artefacts have NO upstream cap -- cargo does not track them yet
  (rust-lang/cargo#13136), and one `target/` per repo across a working tree
  full of them is what actually fills a disk. `build.build-dir` puts the
  intermediates in one central tree, and `hyperi-rust-cache-prune` enforces a
  ceiling over that tree.

Only intermediates move. Each project's `target/` keeps its final binaries, so
`cargo run`, IDEs and anything that globs for a built artefact are unaffected.

What it does NOT do
-------------------
It does not move or symlink existing target/ directories. That was in the
predecessor to this script, and it is deliberately gone: it was site-specific
(it assumed a particular machine's disk layout) and its implementation lost
data on a re-run. `build.build-dir` is cargo's own supported route, stable
since Rust 1.91 -- new builds simply write elsewhere, nothing is relocated, and
a box on an older toolchain just keeps the default layout.

It does not install Rust. Use rustup.
"""

